tic Text Simplification:\nL43: In the Search of a Construct\nL44: ###### Abstract\nL45: Readability is a key concept in the current era of abundant written information. To help making texts more readable and make information more accessible to everyone, a line of researched aims at making texts accessible for their target audience: automatic text simplification (ATS). Lately, there have been studies on the correlations between automatic evaluation metrics in ATS and human judgment.\nL46: However, the correlations between those two aspects and commonly available readability measures (such as readability formulas or linguistic features) have not been the focus of as much attention. In this work, we investigate the place of readability measures in ATS by complementing the existing studies on evaluation metrics and human judgment, on English.\nL47: We first discuss the relationship between ATS and research in readability, then we report a study on correlations between readability measures and human judgment, and between readability measures and ATS evaluation metrics. We identify that in general, readability measures do not correlate well with automatic metrics and human judgment.\nL48: We argue that as the three different angles from which simplification can be assessed tend to exhibit rather low correlations with one another, there is a need for a clear definition of the construct in ATS.\nL49: Keywords: Automatic Text Simplification, Readability, Evaluation\nL50: Rémi Cardon^{1}, A. Seza Doğruöz^{2}\nL51: ^{1}Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Universidad Carlos III de Madrid\nL52: ^{2}LT3 / IDLab, Universiteit Gent\nL53: rcardon@inf.uc3m.es, as.dogruoz@ugent.be\nL54: \nL55: Abstract content\nL56: ## 1.  Introduction\nL57: The accessibility of written information is an important question: outside natural language processing (NLP), domains like medicine cite30†Gu et al. (2024) or business cite31†Huong Dau et al. (2024) have been studying the readability of the documents they produce (e.g. medical reports or information for patients, business reports for shareholders).\nL58: Usually, those studies are performed using traditional readability formulas, like the Flesch Reading Ease cite32†Flesch (1948) or Dale-Chall cite33†Dale and Chall (1948) formulas. Recently, they have been acknowledging the reliability issues that come with those formulas cite34†Alzaid et al. (2024) . In NLP, Automatic text simplification (ATS) is a task that aims at transforming texts in order to make them more accessible, while preserving their meaning cite35†Saggion (2017) .\nL59: In ATS studies, the goal is sometimes phrased as increasing the readability of a text. However, there is a knowledge gap as to how readability measures and judgment on ATS systems (either automated or human) correlate. In this paper, we investigate the place that readability occupies in the ATS landscape. We analyze the discourse on readability in ATS works by putting it in contrast with automatic readability assessment (ARA), that aims at identifying the readability level of texts cite36†Vajjala (2022) .\nL60: While readability is regularly mentioned in current ATS works, ATS does not leverage ARA developments.\nL61: We also study the correlations between readability measures, human judgment and ATS evaluation metrics. While there have been studies about the correlations between ATS evaluation metrics and human judgment cite37†Alva-Manchego et al. (2021) ; cite38†Cripwell et al. (2024) , the correlations between those two aspects and commonly available readability measures have not been the focus of as much attention.\nL62: We fill this gap by reporting a study on correlations between readability measures and human judgment, and between readability measures and ATS evaluation metrics.\nL63: Our contributions are the following: a discussion of ATS and ARA that identifies the bridges that remain to be made between the two fields; experiments with readability measures for ATS evaluation that fill a knowledge gap regarding correlations of evaluation practices and human judgment; insights for future developments of the field of ATS.\nL64: ## 2.  Related Work\nL65: \nL66: In this section, we discuss the fields of readability and text simplification that we introduce separately (Sections cite8†2.1 and cite9†2.2 ) before discussing how the two fields interact with each other (Section cite12†2.3 ).\nL67: ### 2.1.  Readability\nL68: Readability is a field of research that is considered to date back to the 1920’s, with the first attempt to quantify the readability of English texts cite39†Lively and Pressey (1923) . This first method relied on a list of word frequencies cite40†Thorndike (1921) , relying on the assumption that texts made of frequent words are more readable. cite41†François (2015) distinguishes several eras in text readability research, from cite39†Lively and Pressey (1923) to various paradigms of “AI readability\".\nL69: We give an overview of this historical perspective below.\nL70: The early period consisted of identifying predictors and tuning coefficient weights out of corpus-based observations and annotations from a given target audience. The most famous examples for English are Flesch Reading Ease (cite32†Flesch, 1948 , FRE) and Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level (cite42†Kincaid et al., 1975 , FKGL), which rely on word count and number of syllables per word.\nL71: The first approaches to measuring readability with NLP tools relied on linear regression on linguistic (i.e. syntactic and lexical) variables cite43†Daoust et al. (1996) , latent semantic analysis for textual coherence and cohesion cite44†Foltz et al. (1998) and probabilities computed with language modeling cite45†Si and Callan (2001) .\nL72: cite41†François (2015) concludes by noting an emerging trend at the time in ARA, that relies on automatic feature extraction using neural networks. Ten years later, ARA has developed into a lively line of research cite36†Vajjala (2022) . ARA has been explored with distributional text representations and with linguistic features. The distributional text representations follow the advancements of research in machine learning, notably with the development of transformers cite46†Vaswani et al. (2017) .\nL73: Regarding linguistic features, the way to select and leverage them is still an open question. Nonetheless, research on this question is facilitated by the appearance of tools that can be used to compute an increasingly high number of features, for example for English cite47†Kyle et al. (2021) ; cite48†Kyle et al. (2018) ; cite49†Lu (2010) ; cite50†Crossley et al. (2019) or French cite51†Wilkens et al. (2022) .\nL74: These tools produce raw analyses of texts with hundreds of features, with no recommendations as to how to select and use them which is left up to their user. This knowledge gap has fueled research, notably with works that aim at combining those numeric representations with distributional representations cite52†Deutsch et al. (2020) ; cite53†Lee et al. (2021) ; cite54†Wilkens et al. (2024) .\nL75: The readability features depend heavily on the language that is under study. The aforementioned tools rely on language-dependent resource such as reference corpora, vocabulary lists, or pre-trained models (e.g. for POS-tagging or syntactic analysis).\nL76: ### 2.2.  Automatic Text Simplification\nL77: \nL78: In this section, we briefly describe ATS to lay the ground for the discussion of how it integrates considerations about readability that comes in the next section (Section cite12†2.3 ).\nL79: #### Methods\nL80: ATS has traditionally been performed at the sentence-level cite35†Saggion (2017) . In the early works, the goal was to make sentences simpler to handle as an input for other NLP systems such as syntactic parsers cite55†Chandrasekar et al. (1996) . It was only later explored as a means of simplifying texts to make them easier to understand by humans cite56†Carroll et al. (1999) .\nL81: These initial methods were rule-based and targeted specific operations cite57†Cardon and Bibal (2023) (e.g., removing appositive clauses, changing the voice of a sentence from passive to active). The recent developments of generative models has accelerated the shift of ATS research to document-level simplification cite58†Sun et al. (2021) , notably with multi-agent architectures cite59†Mo and Hu (2024) ; cite60†Fang et al. (2025) while sentence simplification is still being explored cite61†Kew et al. (2023) .\nL82: #### Evaluation.\nL83: Evaluation of ATS is an open question. Traditional readability, mostly FKGL or adaptions of FRE for other languages are often reported, while it has been shown that they correlate poorly with human judgment on simplification cite62†Tanprasert and Kauchak (2021) ; cite37†Alva-Manchego et al. (2021) . For sentence simplification, the most common metrics are BLEU cite63†Papineni et al. (2002) , SARI cite64†Xu et al. (2016) , with an adaptation for document-level simplification D-SARI cite58†Sun et al.\nL84: (2021) , and BERTScore cite65†Zhang et al. (2020) . BLEU and BERTScore compare the output to one or more references, while (D-)SARI adds the input into the computation. Their correlation with the task is also unclear cite37†Alva-Manchego et al. (2021) ; cite66†Sulem et al. (2018) , although BLEU is often interpreted as an indicator of meaning preservation, SARI of simplicity, and BERTScore of meaning preservation and fluency.\nL85: These three indicators are the three criteria that are also used for human judgment to evaluate sentence simplification, typically on 5-point Likert scales. For document-level simplification, human evaluation is not stabilized. cite38†Cripwell et al. (2024) use the same criteria with binary questions instead of Likert scales. cite58†Sun et al. (2021) ask judges to evaluate “overall simplicity\" that they define as simplicity with other quality criteria such as ease of reading and meaning preservation.\nL86: cite67†Vásquez-Rodríguez et al. (2023) ask judges to evaluate textual coherence. cite68†Agrawal and Carpuat (2024) evaluate meaning preservation by studying human performance on reading comprehension tests.\nL87: ### 2.3.  Readability and Text Simplification\nL88: In most works where readability and ATS interact, readability is leveraged through linguistic features to give information about datasets cite69†Battisti et al. (2020) ; cite70†Vajjala and Lučić (2018) ; cite71†Yaneva et al. (2016) ; cite72†Štajner and Saggion (2013) ; cite73†Dell’Orletta et al. (2011) ; cite74†Aluisio et al. (2010) . Other research works cite75†Jingshen et al. (2024) rely on features for data selection instead.\nL89: Readability features, in conjunction with similarity measures, are leveraged to mine sentence pairs to produce a parallel corpus for Chinese idiom simplification. cite76†De Martino (2023) investigates the link between eye-tracking data and readability features on Italian data. While it is a preliminary study, it suggests that eye-tracking is a promising method for evaluating the effect of simplification transformations.\nL90: Some ATS studies use readability features or metrics in their evaluation protocol. cite77†Scholz and Wenzel (2025) evaluate 18 readability features (i.e., syntactic, POS-based, semantic and fluency features) for English and German text simplification. Their findings are that some measures are transferable (semantic and fluency features), and that the behavior of statistical, POS-based and syntactic metrics seem to be strongly language-dependent.\nL91: cite78†Paula and Camilo-Junior (2024) use a Portuguese adaption of FRE as an evaluation metric for ATS. cite79†Engelmann et al. (2024) use the FRE and Dale-Chall formulas to perform pairwise comparisons in an Elo-like ranking system. They compare it to human judgments and GPT 3.5 performance. They find that Dale-Chall has the highest correlation to human judgment, above GPT 3.5, while FRE obtains the lowest correlations.\nL92: Readability can also be incorporated in ATS methods. cite80†Flores et al. (2023) use a bounded FKGL (ranging from 4 to 20, based on empirical observations) as a component of their loss in a neural model for text simplification. cite81†Maddela and Alva-Manchego (2025) prompt LLMs for document-level simplification by including CEFR levels in the prompt, as was also done by cite82†Imperial and Tayyar Madabushi (2023) .\nL93: Using CEFR as a proxy for readability was initiated with the release of the CEFR-SP dataset cite83†Arase et al. (2022) , a corpus of 17,000 English sentences annotated with CEFR levels.\nL94: Lexical complexity features have been leveraged for lexical simplification cite84†North et al. (2025) . cite85†Hazim et al. (2022) introduce a system that highlights complex words in a text editor to help humans manually simplify texts. cite86†Maddela and Xu (2018) use lexical features to rank candidates for substitution in a neural lexical simplification system. cite87†Grigonyte et al. (2014) rely on features to perform complex word identification.\nL95: In conclusion, we observe that different approaches to readability (features, formulas, eye-tracking, CEFR levels) are explored in ATS works. The two approaches that are widely present in ATS are traditional formulas, which have consistently been used as an evaluation metric, and readability features, that have been used to give information about datasets. In this study, we explore how features correlate with human judgment on the simplification task, for the English language.\nL96: ## 3.  Readability Measures and ATS Metrics\nL97: ### 3.1.  Data\nL98: In order to study how readability features correlate with the evaluation protocols in ATS, we rely on English data that are labeled with human judgment and on which automatic metrics can be computed. Two studies provide this kind of data, at the sentence level cite37†Alva-Manchego et al. (2021) and at the document level cite81†Maddela and Alva-Manchego (2025) . Both studies aim at studying the link between automatic metrics and human judgment.\nL99: In this paper, we add observations on the link between readability measures and human judgment, and on the link between readability measures and automatic metrics. We describe the datasets below.\nL100: SimplicityDA. For the sentence-level study, we use Simplicity-DA cite37†Alva-Manchego et al. (2021) ^{1}^{1} 1 cite88†https://github.com/feralvam/metaeval-simplification†github.com . It is a set of 600 sentence simplification system outputs in English, each one annotated by 15 crowdworkers along with the three common human judgment criteria ( fluency, simplicity and meaning preservation) in ATS on a 0-100 scale, using the direct assessment method.\nL101: The dataset also includes automatic scores for each sentence: BLEU, SARI, BERTScore and SAMSA.\nL102: D-Wikipedia. For the document-level study, we use D-Wikipedia cite58†Sun et al. (2021) . D-Wikipedia is a corpus of aligned paragraph pairs that come from the English Wikipedia for the complex side and Simple English Wikipedia for the simple side. cite81†Maddela and Alva-Manchego (2025) released a subset of 100 paragraph pairs from D-Wikipedia, each with 4 simplifications produced by automatic systems, resulting in 500 paragraph pairs.\nL103: Those 500 pairs were rated by three human judges on fluency, simplicity and meaning preservation on a 5-point Likert scale. We compute the automatic metrics values with the code provided with the dataset^{2}^{2} 2 cite89†https://github.com/cardiffnlp/document-simplification†github.com . Those automatic metrics are BLEU, SARI, D-SARI, BERTScore and LENS.\nL104: cite81†Maddela and Alva-Manchego (2025) also introduce adaptations of SARI, LENS and BERTScore (respectively Agg-SARI, Agg-LENS and Agg-BERTScore) to the document-level simplification task by aggregating scores computed at the sentence-level.\nL105: ### 3.2.  Readability Measures\nL106: \nL107: cite90†Image: Refer to caption (a) Difference between original and simple,\nL108: SimplicityDA (sentence-level).\nL109: \nL110: cite91†Image: Refer to caption (b) Difference between original and simple,\nL111: D-Wikipedia (document-level).\nL112: \nL113: cite92†Image: Refer to caption (c) Simple side of SimplicityDA (sentence-level).\nL114: \nL115: cite93†Image: Refer to caption (d) Simple side of D-Wikipedia (document-level).\nL116: Figure 1: Pearson correlation matrices of readability measures and metrics. Dashed lines indicate the boundaries of feature groups (from top to bottom, and the same from left to right: TAALES, TAACO, TAASSC, TAALED, and Metrics).\nL117: #### Readability Features.\nL118: \nL119: As discussed in Section cite7†2 , readability is now mostly explored with two types of text representations: distributional embeddings and textual features. As distributional embeddings are leveraged for ATS methods and evaluation, we focus on textual features. To compute these features, we use the four tools (see below to implement a total of 1,066 readability-related features for English:\nL120: \nL121:   * •\nL122: TAALED cite47†Kyle et al. (2021) ^{3}^{3} 3 cite94†https://www.linguisticanalysistools.org/taaled.html†www.linguisticanalysistools.org computes 38 features related to lexical diversity, such as different type-token ratios or MTLD (Measures of Textual Lexical Diversity).\nL123: \nL124:   * •\nL125: TAALES cite48†Kyle et al. (2018) ^{4}^{4} 4 cite95†https://www.linguisticanalysistools.org/taales.html†www.linguisticanalysistools.org computes 484 features related to lexical sophistication. Many of those features are variations around word frequency, computed on various corpora such as BNC (cite96†Consortium, 2007 , The British National Corpus) and COCA (cite97†Davies, 2008 , The Corpus of Contemporary American English) for example. Other features are related to neighborhood (e.g.\nL126: orthographic, phonological), age of acquisition, psycholinguistic norms (e.g. concreteness, imageability, meaningfulness).\nL127:   * •\nL128: \nL129: TAASSC cite49†Lu (2010) ^{5}^{5} 5 cite98†https://www.linguisticanalysistools.org/taassc.html†www.linguisticanalysistools.org computes 376 features related to syntactic sophistication. Those features rely on dependency analysis and part-of-speech tagging. Some examples of features are conjunctions per clause, verbal modifiers per nominal, frequency of constructions compared to references coming from different corpora (e.g. BNC, COCA and others) or more traditional ones such as average sentence length.\nL130:   * •\nL131: \nL132: TAACO cite50†Crossley et al. (2019) ^{6}^{6} 6 cite99†https://www.linguisticanalysistools.org/taaco.html†www.linguisticanalysistools.org computes 168 features related to cohesion. Some examples are semantic similarity between word2vec cite100†Mikolov et al. (2013) embeddings of adjacent sentences, or token overlap between adjacent sentences or paragraphs.\nL133: \nL134: The complete list of features and their formulas is available in the documentation of each tool.\nL135: #### Readability Metrics.\nL136: \nL137: We also compute the following set of traditional readability metrics for English, using the cite101†textstat†pypi.org Python library: Flesch Reading Ease cite32†Flesch (1948) , Dale-Chall cite33†Dale and Chall (1948) , Gunning-Fog cite102†Gunning (1952) , Linsear Write cite103†O’hayre (1966) , ARI cite104†Smith and Senter (1967) , SMOG cite105†Mc Laughlin (1969) , Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level cite42†Kincaid et al. (1975) , and Coleman-Liau cite106†Coleman and Liau (1975) .\nL138: (a) simp – simplicity\nL139: Variable  | $r$\nL140: --- | ---\nL141: